{
  "answer": "No. A recorded supervision event or completed reconciliation may be relevant evidence, but neither alone establishes adequate supervision or proper ownership records. Fulfillment requires an applicable substantive standard and facts showing that the activity met that standard for the relevant actor, product and period. The retained provisions supply qualitative requirements and factors, not an event-based safe harbour.",
  "claims": [
    {
      "inference": "The text distinguishes continuing responsibility from performance evidence. Deduction: recording an activity does not establish that the Provider fulfilled its responsibility for operational soundness and ownership records.",
      "missing_premises": [
        "Applicability of the retained edition and any product-specific conditions, amendments or relaxations.",
        "The entity's Product Provider role for the relevant product, activity and assessment period.",
        "An applicable standard for operational soundness and proper ownership records, and evidence satisfying it."
      ],
      "proposition": "Ultimate responsibility persists despite outsourcing; neither an outsourcing allocation nor a recorded oversight event proves fulfillment.",
      "status": "Text-supported requirement; fulfillment not established",
      "support": [
        {
          "quote": "Product Providers should remain and be ultimately responsible for the management and operational soundness of the tokenisation arrangement adopted and record keeping of ownership, regardless of any outsourcing arrangement1.",
          "unit_id": "d3b41d4d7e61c.u38.3432"
        },
        {
          "quote": "the Product Provider should remain fully responsible, despite any outsourcing arrangement such as those envisaged under 6.8 of the Code on Unit Trusts and Mutual Funds.",
          "unit_id": "d3b41d4d7e61c.u85.7210"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "inference": "UT 6.11C expressly addresses ongoing monitoring, supervision and periodic review. Deduction: a single logged event establishes, at most, an occurrence if the record is reliable; it does not establish continuity or adequacy. Applying this valuation-specific provision to generic tokenisation supervision would require a separate legal justification.",
      "missing_premises": [
        "The UT provision applies, the entity is the management company, and a third party performs the scheme's valuation.",
        "A context-specific assessment of reasonable care, skill and diligence and appropriate supervision and review.",
        "Reliable evidence of the supervision's scope, frequency, substance, reviewer competence, findings and follow-up throughout the relevant period.",
        "Evidence that the third party has the requisite knowledge, experience and resources."
      ],
      "proposition": "A recorded supervision event alone does not establish fulfillment of UT 6.11C's valuation-specific requirements.",
      "status": "Text-supported standard; conditional deduction",
      "support": [
        {
          "quote": "Where a third party is engaged in the valuation of a scheme, the management company shall exercise reasonable care, skill and diligence in the selection, appointment and ongoing monitoring of such third party in ensuring such entity possesses the appropriate level of knowledge, experience and resources that commensurate with the appropriate valuation policies and procedures for each scheme.",
          "unit_id": "ut-current.parent"
        },
        {
          "quote": "The valuation activities of such third party should be subject to ongoing supervision and periodic review by the management company.",
          "unit_id": "ut-current.parent"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "inference": "Paragraph 11 requires proper records of ownership interests and operational compatibility. The appendix treats regular reconciliation as one consideration alongside legal validity and encumbrances. Deduction: completion of reconciliation does not establish that the records accurately reflect legally effective interests; matching records could share an error.",
      "missing_premises": [
        "The applicable legal meaning of ownership interests, including governing law, title structure, settlement finality and encumbrances.",
        "A justified standard for record accuracy, completeness, timeliness, reconciliation frequency and discrepancy resolution.",
        "Evidence identifying the records compared, their reliability, coverage, reconciliation results and resolution of exceptions.",
        "Evidence of continued record maintenance and operational compatibility with involved service providers."
      ],
      "proposition": "A completed reconciliation is relevant evidence, not a sufficient test of proper ownership records.",
      "status": "Text-supported requirement and factor; sufficiency rejected by deduction",
      "support": [
        {
          "quote": "Product Providers should ensure that proper records of token holders’ ownership interests in the product are maintained and the tokenisation arrangement is operationally compatible with service providers involved2.",
          "unit_id": "d3b41d4d7e61c.u41.3664"
        },
        {
          "quote": "whether the ownership interest of the underlying assets of the Tokenised Securities are legally valid under the applicable governing laws, whether there are regular reconciliations between the records for the extrinsic rights and the records for the distributed ledger tokens, or whether any encumbrance affects the extrinsic rights.",
          "unit_id": "tokenised-securities.appendix"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "inference": "The appendix expressly supplies non-exhaustive considerations, incorporated through footnote 1 only where applicable. Judgment: the proposed factual checks above are evidential questions for applying the qualitative standards, not a source-prescribed exhaustive checklist or automatic pass rule.",
      "missing_premises": [
        "A reasoned determination of which appendix factors apply to the actual arrangement.",
        "Any further applicable authorisation conditions or regulatory requirements.",
        "An assessment connecting actual evidence to the substantive standard, rather than equating a logged event with compliance."
      ],
      "proposition": "Neither the candidate rules nor acceptance by a formal solver supplies the missing legal standard or factual proof.",
      "status": "Methodological deduction; legal correctness not certified",
      "support": [
        {
          "quote": "Part A: List of non-exhaustive factors for intermediaries’ consideration",
          "unit_id": "tokenised-securities.appendix"
        },
        {
          "quote": "For factors of consideration, please also refer to Part A of the Appendix to the Tokenised Securities Circular, where applicable, when formulating the tokenisation arrangement.",
          "unit_id": "d3b41d4d7e61c.u85.7210"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "legal_correctness_established": false,
  "question_id": "evidence",
  "rival_readings": [
    "An event may prove the narrow fact that supervision or reconciliation occurred, assuming a reliable record. It does not thereby prove substantive adequacy.",
    "A conduct-focused assessment would examine competent, ongoing supervision and follow-up; an outcome-focused assessment would examine sound operations and proper records. A combined assessment may require both. The retained text does not establish that any one approach alone conclusively discharges paragraph 10.",
    "UT 6.11C supplies an express standard for outsourced valuation. Using it by analogy for other tokenisation activities remains distinct from establishing its direct applicability."
  ],
  "unresolved": [
    "No actual supervision or reconciliation evidence is supplied.",
    "The applicable entity roles, product, period, authorisation conditions and substantive adequacy thresholds remain unestablished.",
    "The retained text does not specify a universal supervision frequency, reconciliation tolerance or sufficient evidential package.",
    "Duty applicability and fulfillment remain separate determinations; no finding of compliance or breach follows from the supplied event descriptions alone."
  ]
}
